<section xml:id="section-domain-hierarcy">
  <title>The Hierarchy of Domains</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-euclidean-domain">
    <p>
      I ended the last section knowing that the Gaussian integers have a
      division algorithm. How does this help? In this section, I want to
      prove two results that show now useful a division algorithm can
      be. First, however, a definition.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p> 
          Let <m>R</m> be a domain. If <m>R</m> possess a norm
          <m>N</m> and a division algorithm (in the sense of <xref
          ref="theorem-division-algorithm"/>, then <m>R</m> is called
          a <term>Euclidean domain</term>. (A division algorithm is
          often also called a <sq>Euclidean algorithm</sq>, explaining
          the term.) 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      For our purposes, the most important property of the Euclidean
      algorithm is this proposition.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> be an Euclidean domain. Then every ideal in
          <m>R</m> is principal. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>N</m> be the norm associated with the division
          algorithm in <m>R</m>. Then take any ideal <m>I \subset
          R</m>. If <m>I</m> contains a unit <m>u</m>, then <m>I = R</m> and
          can be written as <m>I = \langle u \rangle</m>, since any
          unit generates the whole ring. Therefore, I'll assume
          <m>I</m> is a proper ideal.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>a, b \in I</m>. I can apply the division algorithm to
          produce <m>r = a - bq</m>. Since <m>a, b \in I</m>, I know
          that <m>pq</m> is also an ideal element (by the ideal
          property that all multiples of any ideal element stay in the
          ideal). Therefore <m>r</m> is also in the ideal and is a
          smaller element in the idea: <m>N(r) \leq N(b)</m>. In this
          way, by repeating the division algorithm, I can get a
          sequence of elements of the ideal all of which have smaller
          norms than the previous. Eventually this has to stop (any
          sequence of decreasing integers eventually reaches zero).
          Let <m>p</m> be the smallest norm element (non-zero)
          in this sequence. 
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I claim that <m>I = \langle p \rangle</m>. Take any
          other element <m>c \in I</m>. I can again apply the division
          algorith to get <m>c = pq + r</m> with <m>N(r) \lt N(p)</m>.
          But since the norm of <m>p</m> is minimal (and since the
          ideal is proper and I cannot generate a unit), I conclude that 
          <m>N(r) = 0</m>. Only zero has a zero norm, so I conclude
          <m>c = pq</m> and <m>c</m> is a multiple of <m>p</m>. This
          shows that any ideal element is a multiple of <m>p</m>,
          which means the ideal is principle with generator <m>p</m>.
          This completes the proof. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-pids">
    <title>Prinical Ideal Domains</title>
    <p>
      There is a name for this kind of domain as well. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> be a domain where all ideals are principal.
          Then <m>R</m> is called a <term>principal ideal
          domain</term>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      I've talked about the relationship between prime numbers and
      prime ideals. Now that can be formalized. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> be a principal ideal domain. An element <m>p
          \in R</m> is called <term>prime</term> if <m>\langle p
          \rangle</m> is a prime ideal 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      In principal ideal domains, ideals match up with elements (at
      least up to multiplication by units, that is). Talking about
      prime <sq>numbers</sq> makes sense here, since going between
      those elements and their ideals is seamless. This is why, in the
      Gaussian integers, I talked about prime elements. The Gaussian
      integers are a Eucidean domain, so they are also a principal
      ideal domain, so prime elements makes sense. I will show you
      examples where this fails and we do actually need to talk about
      prime ideas instead of prime elements. 
    </p>
    <p>
      For our purposes, the main reason to care about principal ideal
      domains (other than the convenience of having prime element make
      sense) is the following proposition. I'm not going to prove this
      proposition, since that would involve some technical details
      which we haven't set up in this course. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every principle ideal domain is also a unique factorization
          domain. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>The Hierarchy</title>
    <p>
      I can summarize what is going on in this section with a
      hierarchy of domains. Here is a list, from the most restrictive
      condition to the least.
      <ul>
        <li>
          The most restrictive class is fields. In these rings,
          everything (other than zero) is invertible. There are no
          ideals, hence no primes and factoring questions at all. 
        </li>
        <li>
          All fields are Euclidean domains, which have a division
          algorithm. In these rings, gcd and lcms can be calculated
          with the algorithm, as can prime decompositions.
        </li>
        <li>
          All Euclidean domains are principal ideals domains. In these
          domains, prime elements and prime ideals match up.
          Factorization into prime elements makes sense. However,
          calculating factors can be difficult, since the ring may
          lack a division algorithm. 
        </li>
        <li>
          All principal ideal domains are unique factorization
          domains. Having principal ideals is enough to guarantee that
          if an element factors into primes, there is only one way to
          do so. 
        </li>
        <li>
          Not all domains are unique factorization domain. Again, we
          will see an example shortly. 
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
